#
# A filter whose selection moved DOWN a level rather than to nothing. `%p` holds one map whose `Name`
# key is also a key of its `Inner` entry. In the previous release `%p[ Name exists ]` tested the map
# itself and selected it; it now walks the map's entries and selects `Inner`. Both rules below read
# past the filter, so what they compare moved with the selection:
#
# - `.Tags exists`: the map had no `Tags` and neither does `Inner`, so the clause fails either way, but
#   the thing it failed on changed.
# - `.Name == 'b'`: the map's `Name` is `a` (FAIL before); `Inner`'s is `b`, and the `Name` string entry
#   is a scalar the filter passes over, so the clause compares `Inner`'s `b` alone and passes (for one
#   revision the string was reported unresolved beside `Inner` and failed the comparison).
#
# Each filter is announced once: the predicate holds on the map and the map is not among what the
# filter selected. A gate that read only the end of the whole query announced the first (every result
# unresolved) with the wrong words and missed the second (a resolved result after the filter).
#
let p = Resources.B.Properties

rule MOVED_DOWN_TAGS {
  %p[ Name exists ].Tags exists
}

rule MOVED_DOWN_NAME {
  %p[ Name exists ].Name == 'b'
}

# Both "before" verdicts were measured on binaries built from the 3.1.12, 3.2.0 and 3.2.1 release commits over
# two-level-properties-template.yaml: MOVED_DOWN_NAME FAIL and MOVED_DOWN_TAGS FAIL, with no notice; the
# "now" verdicts (NAME PASS, TAGS FAIL, one notice per site) are what the test over this file asserts. The
# note sits after the rules so the notices' line numbers, which the test pins, stay where they were.
